sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch13-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Extinction has defined and widened the intervals between groups; even the distinctness of whole classes, such as birds from other vertebrates, results from the loss of many ancient forms that once connected their early progenitors.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Gaps between groups, even whole classes, are due to extinction of connecting forms";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter XIII, “Mutual Affinities of Organic Beings: Morphology: Embryology: Rudimentary Organs”; section “Extinction separates and defines groups”. Scope: classes and other higher groups, especially vertebrate classes"
}
